  "body": "Given the following HTML, taken from https://github.com/html5lib/html5lib-tests/blob/master/tree-construction/tests16.dat#L2590:\r\n\r\n```html\r\n<!doctype html><form><table></form><form></table></form>\r\n```\r\n\r\nDuring \"in table insertion mode\"'s \"A start tag whose tag name is \"form\"\" steps:\r\n* The second &lt;form&gt; element is inserted in to the DOM\r\n* The `form element pointer` is set to the newly created <form> element\r\n* The newly created <form> element is popped off the stack\r\n\r\nThen in \"in body insertion mode\"'s \"An end tag whose tag name is \"form\"\" steps:\r\n* `node` is set to the `form element pointer`, which is the second &lt;form&gt; element.\r\n* The `form element pointer` is then set to null\r\n* `node` is non-null, so we eventually get to step 6\r\n* Step 6 says to remove `node` from the stack, but `node` was already removed from during \"in table insertion mode\"'s `A start tag whose tag name is \"form\"` steps.\r\n\r\nThe spec seems to imply that `node` should be on the stack of open elements when it encounters it's end tag, as it just says to \"Remove node from the stack of open elements.\", however, node is not on the stack because it was popped off immediately after being created. It seems like most of the cases of removing a node from the stack is guarded by first checking if the node is on the stack before removing. Am I interpreting this wrong?",
